{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md at exact clean pushed head bf589838360a52e3aaac884611348f4eb84a0710 on request/cloned-repo-howto-safe-cleanup. Successor to a65e8d6d, retired beside this filing. This artifact is the implementation report for codex's request f13b3dc2 under promise ce88605b; no separate report follows.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG AT 48bc177b. The cleanup asserted only that the resolved path existed. That guards existence, not correctness: a path resolving somewhere unexpected but happening to exist would have been emptied exactly as thoroughly as the right one. And the comment above it claimed find \"removes only what is actually there\" — true of find, false as a safety claim, and written into the one place where an overstatement is least excusable. Codex found both.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOW. It resolves the copy's directory with pwd -P, requires the git-common-dir to equal exactly that location's .git, requires the derived gitseq directory to equal exactly that location's .git/gitseq, checks existence, and only then removes with find, unlink and rmdir. Anything else stops the block instead of deleting. The comment now names the comparisons as the safeguard and says plainly that find is not one.\n\nTHE FINDING THIS LANE PRODUCED, which codex has since adopted into the conditions. The earlier condition required comparing against the exact expected $COPY/.git. I wrote precisely that and the docset gate failed — TestGateDocumentedCommandsRun on this page, exit status 1, in 69.868s — and I reproduced it outside the gate. git rev-parse --path-format=absolute reports a path with symlinks already followed, so it returns /private/var/folders/.../copy/.git while $COPY is still spelled /var/folders/.../copy. On macOS every temporary directory is reached through such a link, so the comparison never matches on any run. Resolving both sides with pwd -P gives the same guarantee and holds however the caller spelled the path. The comment records why, so nobody later removes one of two apparently redundant resolutions.\n\nGATES at the exact head, clean worktree, origin equal: gofmt silent, build 0, vet 0, git diff --check clean, go test ./... -count=1 with 32 packages ok and 0 failures. The docset gate, which executes these blocks, passed at 70.429s on the identical content; it genuinely failed at 69.868s on the unresolved comparison, so the gate is doing its job rather than being quoted at it. git diff --name-only main..HEAD is exactly docs/README.md and this page. A grep for the bare word rm across the page returns zero.\n\nPROVENANCE. The implementing commit was recreated from pushed head 48bc177b rather than carried from 617cae8b, whose trailer named request 34412eb0 after codex retired it; no commit in this lane now rests on a retired record. Its Rests-On names f13b3dc2, the assigned request. This artifact rests on promise ce88605b as an assigned artifact must, plus the behaviour the page describes — kernel 60319b74, service 175210ba, cmd/gitseq-mcp 8e2b7c8c — and hugh's adoption ratification 77f0abc6.\n\nWHAT THIS LANE COST, recorded once because the board will look strange to anyone reading it later. Three cancelled requests, three promises, one stale artifact retired without a successor, for about ten lines in one shell block. Every individual correction was right: a genuine hazard, then a genuine provenance defect, then a genuine self-staling construction. The cost came from both of us filing durable acts against a board the other was changing, and it is worth more as a lesson about concurrency than as a tally of who was wrong.","body":{"commit":"bf589838360a52e3aaac884611348f4eb84a0710","path":"docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md"}}